<p>
This tool reads no build-time data of its own beyond two small index files. Everything it reports is decoded from
the schematics themselves, in your browser, when you search.
</p>
<h3>What is fetched</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>assets/schematicSearchIndex.json</code> &mdash; each schematic's <code>BlockCodes</code> and <code>ItemCodes</code> values (the same lists the
<a href="{{ ('/' ~ lang ~ '/') | url }}sources/schematics/">schematic pages</a> are built from). A schematic can only contain the searched code, or a random
placeholder that can produce it, if one of those lists mentions it, so this exactly decides which files need downloading.</li>
<li><code>assets/randomizerPools.json</code> &mdash; every <code>ItemStackRandomizer</code> item's pool (the item pages' "Can generate" data), to know which
<code>stackrandomizer-*</code> placeholders can turn into the searched item.</li>
<li>The candidate schematics' gzip copies (<code>assets/schematics/world/&lt;domain&gt;/&lt;path&gt;.json.gz</code>), unpacked with <code>DecompressionStream</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What is reported</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Placed</strong>: blocks in <code>Indices</code>/<code>BlockIds</code> whose code matches.</li>
<li><strong>Chiseled from</strong>: chiseled blocks (block entities with <code>cuboids</code> and <code>materials</code>, see the research page) with a matching material.</li>
<li><strong>In containers</strong>: item stacks found anywhere in a block entity's decoded attributes (chests, shelves, ground storage, ...), resolved through
<code>ItemCodes</code> (or <code>BlockCodes</code> for block stacks).</li>
<li><strong>Possible (random)</strong>: stacks that are a randomizer placeholder whose pool contains the target. The percentage is the target's share of
that placeholder's pool; the placeholder's own <code>totalChance</code> attribute is shown unchanged. Whether the game actually generates it is decided at
world-generation time, so this only says it can.</li>
</ul>
<p>Matching ignores the domain and is case-insensitive; <code>*</code> and <code>{...}</code> match anything.</p>
<p>Not covered: contents generated by code rather than by schematics (loot tables of other world-generation systems), and anything only present in
block-entity types that store stacks in a form other than an attribute item stack.</p>
